    "result_text": "Creating an owner with an email another owner already uses now returns **409 Conflict**. The comparison is case-insensitive, so `Foo@X.com` is a duplicate of `foo@x.com`. All 5 tests pass, including `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsDuplicateEmail` (`./mvnw test` \u2192 BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n**What changed:**\n- **Controller** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`): the email is lower-cased once, right after the telephone duplicate check. If it's already in use, the new `DuplicateEmailException` is thrown. Owners created without an email are not checked.\n- **Error response** (`ExceptionControllerAdvice`): a new handler returns 409 with a standard error body that includes an `email` property. It's built the same way as the existing duplicate-telephone handler.\n- **Service and data access**: added `ClinicService.ownerEmailExists` and `OwnerRepository.existsByEmailIgnoringCase`, implemented in all three data-access versions (JDBC, JPA, Spring Data JPA). The queries compare against `LOWER(email)`, so existing rows stored in mixed case are still caught.\n- **API docs** (`openapi.yml`): the description of the create endpoint's 409 response now mentions email.\n\n**Worth knowing:**\n- **Only one data-access version is tested.** The test suite runs only the default setup, so the JDBC and plain-JPA versions of the new query compile but no test exercises them.\n- **Updates are not checked.** As specified, only creating an owner is rejected. Changing an existing owner's email (`updateOwner`) to one that's already taken still succeeds, which matches how the telephone check behaves today.",
